WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.003
- The thing that I remember the
most from that Super Bowl, man,

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:05.588
was the fact that I almost
didn't even get to play in it.

00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:06.798
- What happened in the
NFC Championship Game?

00:00:06.798 --> 00:00:10.635
- In the NFC Championship game, second
quarter—you work your whole life

00:00:10.635 --> 00:00:14.639
for the opportunity to play in the Super
Bowl, right? And to get to that point,

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:18.143
go to make a tackle against
the tight end, and he jumps up;

00:00:18.143 --> 00:00:22.439
the first thing I do was put my arm
out, and his knee caught my arm,

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:24.232
and my arm lost that battle. 

00:00:24.232 --> 00:00:30.947
♪♪♪

00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:32.699
- [Master Tesfatsion:] So who
we got in the pool hall today?

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:33.825
Thomas Davis. What's up, baby?

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:34.451
- [Thomas Davis]: What’s good man?

00:00:34.451 --> 00:00:35.493
- How retirement treating you, man?

00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:36.494
- I can't complain.

00:00:36.494 --> 00:00:37.746
- How nice are you on
the pool stick, man?

00:00:37.746 --> 00:00:39.497
- I mean, I'm all right. I ain't the best, but

00:00:39.497 --> 00:00:42.250
I been watching the show, so I definitely
know that I can take you down.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.252
- Man, go ahead, bro.
Let's see what you can do.

00:00:44.252 --> 00:00:46.337
- Let's see, man. I can't mess this up.

00:00:46.337 --> 00:00:47.130
- [Laughing] 

00:00:47.756 --> 00:00:48.798
There you go.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:50.967
We’ve never had that happen before.

00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:53.136
- Eight on the break! Man, that's a, that's— 
- We’ve never had that happen before!

00:00:53.136 --> 00:00:54.179
- That's a win, baby!

00:00:54.179 --> 00:00:55.430
- A win? That's a L!

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:56.514
- That's a win!

00:00:56.514 --> 00:00:57.223
- Where you from?

00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:57.849
- From Georgia.

00:00:57.849 --> 00:01:00.310
- This must be some, some Georgia-Texas differences, man. 
- It's a dub. That's a dub.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:01.311
- Nah, bro.

00:01:01.311 --> 00:01:04.481
- Hey, make sure y'all keep, make
sure y'all keep that. I need that.

00:01:04.481 --> 00:01:05.440
- What part of Georgia you from?

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.069
- From South Georgia, man, down...a
little small town. Shellman, Georgia.

00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:11.738
Population probably about 900 people.

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:14.949
Not a lot of people know about
Shellman. It's like south of Atlanta.

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:17.952
- So playing high school, I mean,
how many offers you get, then?

00:01:17.952 --> 00:01:19.287
Being in a small town like that.

00:01:19.287 --> 00:01:22.582
- Funny you ask that. So in high school
I played eight different positions. 

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.125
- It wasn't nine-man or
nothing? Or was, was it—

00:01:24.125 --> 00:01:25.627
- Nah, it was 11-man football.

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:26.336
- [Laughing] OK.

00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:29.839
- But it was one of those things where
I was the best player on the team, and

00:01:29.839 --> 00:01:33.551
Coach wanted to put me in the position to
be successful. So eight different positions.

00:01:33.551 --> 00:01:37.388
And when I say that to people, people think,
like, “OK, it was eight different positions

00:01:37.388 --> 00:01:39.057
over the course of your high school career.”

00:01:39.057 --> 00:01:42.060
No. It was eight different
positions per game.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.604
- Just like that Spice Adams, talking
about linebacker, what else you play?

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:47.899
- Exactly. I line up at receiver. I play
quarterback. I play running back.

00:01:47.899 --> 00:01:50.652
 And I did all of those things. I
was the kicker. I was the punter.

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:55.281
I was the punt returner. I think doing
that may have ended up hurting me

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:59.077
more than it helped me because teams
didn't really know how to recruit me,

00:01:59.077 --> 00:02:02.080
where they would play me
at. But I got an offer from

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.291
a Georgia coach coming in,
watching me practice basketball. 

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:08.128
- Damn. Having an offer from
Georgia, being in Georgia.

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:12.006
- You know, being a hometown kid, you
grew up, you watching the hometown team.

00:02:12.006 --> 00:02:15.176
And to get that offer, I, I really
didn't even know how to take it.

00:02:15.176 --> 00:02:17.262
It was some doubt that started to creep in.

00:02:17.262 --> 00:02:20.431
But once I got there, you
know, I made my mind up that

00:02:20.431 --> 00:02:22.350
nobody at Georgia was gonna outwork me.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.061
So I went from the last
guy given a scholarship

00:02:25.061 --> 00:02:28.773
to the first guy drafted out of the 30
kids that went to Georgia that year.

00:02:30.108 --> 00:02:32.235
- You got 16 despite the fact

00:02:32.235 --> 00:02:35.113
that you had to overcome three torn ACL
surgeries in three consecutive seasons. 

00:02:35.113 --> 00:02:39.242
- That’s why I feel like, you know, when
we made it to the Super Bowl in 2015,

00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:42.453
that was supposed to be the year
that kind of capped off everything

00:02:42.453 --> 00:02:44.873
that had already happened
over the course of my career.

00:02:44.873 --> 00:02:47.333
- Take me through that Super Bowl.
Was it Super Bowl 50, right? 

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:47.917
- Yes.

00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:52.338
- Levi Stadium. Panthers. Broncos.
You're facing Peyton Manning.

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:53.756
What do you remember from that night?

00:02:53.756 --> 00:02:56.467
- The thing that I remember the
most from that Super Bowl, man,

00:02:56.467 --> 00:02:59.179
was the fact that I almost
didn't even get to play in it.

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:00.430
- What happened in the
NFC Championship game?

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:03.433
- In the NFC Championship game,
man, in the second quarter—

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:08.271
you work your whole life for the opportunity 
to play in the Super Bowl, right, as a player.

00:03:08.271 --> 00:03:12.942
And for me it was 11 years at that
point. And to get to that point,

00:03:12.942 --> 00:03:17.697
and in the second quarter go to
make a tackle against the tight end.

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:21.743
Me and Luke are closing in
on him, and he jumps up, and

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:26.706
the first thing I do was put my arm
out. And his knee caught my arm,

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:31.336
and my arm lost that battle. I broke my arm.
Trainers come out. They asked me like,

00:03:31.336 --> 00:03:33.838
"What are you thinking?" I'm like,
"Man, it's broken.” We go in the back.

00:03:33.838 --> 00:03:37.967
Everything that we know about injuries
and know about having broken bones,

00:03:37.967 --> 00:03:43.223
there's no way that in two weeks I'm gonna
be able to play in the Super Bowl

00:03:43.223 --> 00:03:43.681
- None.

00:03:43.681 --> 00:03:46.809
- after breaking my arm. But then
we go in at halftime, we go look at it,

00:03:46.809 --> 00:03:50.188
and they were like, "Look, we can
do surgery at six in the morning

00:03:50.188 --> 00:03:54.525
and you can play in the Super Bowl, but it's
really gonna come down to a pain tolerance

00:03:54.525 --> 00:03:56.694
on whether or not you can
play the whole game."

00:03:56.694 --> 00:03:57.737
- When was this conversation happening?

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:59.197
- It was literally at halftime. And then—

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:01.032
- Halftime of the NFC Championship 
Game. Who you guys playing?

00:04:01.032 --> 00:04:04.953
- We played against the Arizona Cardinals.
We go in after we won the game, and they

00:04:04.953 --> 00:04:08.873
and they reassured me that, "Hey, if you can 
deal with the pain of what's gonna come

00:04:08.873 --> 00:04:13.294
after you have this surgery, you can play in
 the Super Bowl." That's all I needed to hear.

00:04:13.294 --> 00:04:15.421
- So next morning you have
the surgery in Charlotte?

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:16.422
- Yes.

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:19.717
- Recovery, everything, and then from that
point on what's the rehab process like?

00:04:19.717 --> 00:04:22.637
- The portion of my arm that
I broke was the ulna, and—

00:04:22.637 --> 00:04:23.554
- You can still see the scar, too.

00:04:23.554 --> 00:04:28.101
- Yeah. The scar's still there. Like, and I
still had this little tissue that they cut into.

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:32.563
So the portion that they cut into was,
like, what really controlled your grip.

00:04:32.563 --> 00:04:35.733
That was the hardest thing that I had to
work through over the course of those

00:04:35.733 --> 00:04:40.363
two weeks to get the grip strength back. We
go from that doubt and that uncertainty of

00:04:40.363 --> 00:04:43.283
being able to play in that Super
Bowl, to not missing the snap.

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:44.993
Like, which is, which is unheard of. 

00:04:44.993 --> 00:04:47.996
- At one of the most physical, if not the
most physical, position of the game.

00:04:47.996 --> 00:04:52.041
- Once the opportunity presented itself,
I'm out there, I'm playin' in this game,

00:04:52.041 --> 00:04:55.169
and we just gonna see what happens.
Defensively, we had a great game, man.

00:04:55.169 --> 00:04:59.299
We had a great game plan, and we
playin' against arguably one of the best

00:04:59.299 --> 00:05:02.885
quarterbacks of all time, and
he didn't beat us. I'll say that.

00:05:02.885 --> 00:05:05.596
He didn't beat us. They won the
game, but he didn't beat us.

00:05:05.596 --> 00:05:08.057
- I had $500 moneyline on y'all, bro.

00:05:08.057 --> 00:05:08.975
- Von Miller.

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:09.642
- Bro.

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:12.478
- DeMarcus Ware. Hats off to
those dudes, man. I told Von,

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:14.689
"Man, you got my Super Bowl
ring," every time I see him.

00:05:14.689 --> 00:05:16.858
- [Laughing]
- Give him a hard time, man.

00:05:16.858 --> 00:05:21.988
♪♪♪

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:23.531
